<title>USA: High Plains Wheat - Heavy rains hit Oklahoma.</title>
<headline>High Plains Wheat - Heavy rains hit Oklahoma.</headline>
<dateline>KANSAS CITY, Mo. 1997-07-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Extremely heavy rains fell overnight across central and northern Oklahoma, grain dealers said Friday.</p>
<p>Precipitation maps published Friday showed that as much as 15 inches of rain fell during a 24-hour period in northern Oklahoma. An Oklahoma grain dealer said he knew of one area that received 17 to 18 inches of rain overnight.</p>
<p>Dealers said basis bids for hard red winter wheat were mostly unchanged Friday, although some firmness was reported at scattered terminals in Texas and Oklahoma. Movement was slow to moderate, dealers said.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, the KCBT clearing house said Friday that Farmland Industries had posted more wheat for delivery against the KCBT July HRW wheat contract. Farmland will deliver 590,000 bushels of wheat Monday to Hutchinson, Kan.</p>
<p>Benson-Quinn Commodities accepted 315,000 bushels and Continental Grain Co will accept the other 275,000.</p>
<p>The latest delivery comes on top of the 1.990 million bushels Farmland delivered Thursday.  </p>
